---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ESSAYS, HOW_PATH, SITE_LAB, adrUrl, howUrl } from '../../lib/links';

// The essay the breaker principle maps to (docs/domain.md, "The principle map"; ADR 0010 decision
// 2.4; docs/roadmap.md, PR 17 and PR 18). The principle has no lab page yet, so the bakery explains
// it itself. Static, no island. Every claim about Cloudflare’s behaviour is as its documentation
// stated it on the date in READ, and every number from the code names its file. The breaker has
// been proven against a fake of Cloudflare’s two APIs and not yet against Cloudflare.
const READ = '2026-11-06';
const { title } = ESSAYS.breaker;
const SOURCES = [
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/observability/metrics-and-analytics/',
    title: 'Metrics and analytics',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Workers documentation',
    for: 'a slight delay in aggregation and metrics delivery for the most recent minutes, which is why the breaker reads the slot before the current one',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/observability/metrics/',
    title: 'Queues metrics',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Queues documentation',
    for: 'the queueMessageOperationsAdaptiveGroups dataset and its billableOperations field, the sum of billable writes, reads and deletes',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/observability/graphql-analytics/',
    title: 'GraphQL analytics for Durable Objects',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'the request dataset, and for the periodic dataset only cpuTime documented, with introspection as the way to list the rest',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/ruleset-engine/rulesets-api/update-rule/',
    title: 'Update a rule',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Ruleset Engine documentation',
    for: 'a rule changed by a PATCH that should carry all the rule fields wanted in the new definition',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/fundamentals/api/reference/permissions/',
    title: 'API token permissions',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s API documentation',
    for: 'the names Account Analytics Read and Zone WAF Edit, with Zone WAF Write in a second table, so that the names are confirmed on the first real run',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://pages.johnspurlock.com/graphql-schema-docs/cloudflare.html',
    title: 'Cloudflare GraphQL schema docs',
    by: 'a third-party introspection dump, not Cloudflare’s own page',
    for: 'the field names rowsWritten and duration for the periodic dataset, which the breaker treats as unread until a real run confirms them',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CircuitBreaker.html',
    title: 'CircuitBreaker',
    by: 'a bliki entry',
    for: 'a protected call wrapped in an object that trips when failures pass a threshold, answers at once while open, and is watched and reset by people',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/IHaveASegway/bakery/blob/main/docs/cost.md',
    title: 'The bakery’s cost.md',
    by: 'this repository',
    for: 'the price sheet, the lines, scenario D, the idle month and the items still open on the first real run',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Proof of Concept Bakery`}
  description="How Proof of Concept Bakery watches its own bill: a cron job that reads Cloudflare’s usage meters every 15 minutes, prices them, and moves the demo to read-only and then off, with a person to switch it back on."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The Workers Paid plan has no spending cap, so a bug can run up a bill that nobody sees for a
      day. This is how the bakery reads the platform’s own meter every 15 minutes, prices what it
      finds, and turns itself down in two steps. It has met only a fake so far.
    </p>

    <h2>A bill that arrives after the money is gone</h2>
    <p>
      Two five-figure Cloudflare bills of 2026 are cited in <code>docs/cost.md</code>. In one, a
      Durable Object alarm re-armed itself for the current time and ran about 600 times a second for
      about two weeks, a $10,811.41 invoice. In the other, a queue consumer re-enqueued its own
      writes, and the bill came to about $36,000. Both rest on their developers’ own posts. The
      bakery holds both shapes, because its aggregates run on alarms and a Queue joins them.
    </p>

    <h2>What a simpler design does, and where it breaks</h2>
    <p>
      The simple design sets budget alerts. The bakery has them, at $10, $25, $50 and $100, and
      treats a ring as a guardrail failing, but an alert gives notice within a day. At the rate of
      the smallest loop <code>docs/cost.md</code> prices, $4.65 an hour, a day of it is $111.60,
      over the $100 ceiling. The second is a cap on what the code admits, which the bakery also has:{' '}
      <code>Control</code> refuses sandboxes past 300 a day. That cap counts what passes through the
      door. A looping alarm never uses the door, so it spends without being counted. The{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        lab’s index
      </a>{' '}
      is where the lab covers the family of techniques, and this principle has no lab page yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      The breaker is the third layer, and it measures what neither of the others can: what the
      platform itself says was used. <em>Release It!</em> set the pattern down, and the
      CircuitBreaker bliki entry describes its shape: it trips when failures pass a threshold,
      answers at once while open, and is reset by people. Here the count is dollars, and what trips
      is the whole demo.
    </p>

    <h2>Five meters, two lines, one decision</h2>
    <p>
      Every 15 minutes the API Worker sends four GraphQL requests to Cloudflare’s Analytics API, one
      per dataset, with a token of its own. They return five meters: Worker requests, Durable Object
      requests, rows written, Durable Object duration in gigabyte-seconds, and Queue operations. The
      window is the last complete 15-minute slot, and each count is priced in micro-dollars at the
      Paid plan’s overage rates, as if every unit were past the included amount. That is
      deliberately cautious.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two lines come from numbers the bakery already has. The dollar line is the $100 ceiling
      divided by the 2,880 windows in a month, $0.0347, the rate that would spend the ceiling in a
      month. Each meter’s line is four times its 15-minute share of the day’s budget, which is{' '}
      <code>Control</code>’s cap of 300 sandboxes times the per-sandbox meter in{' '}
      <code>docs/cost.md</code>: 1,875 Worker requests and 62,500 rows written, for example. The
      Worker does not judge. It sends the reads to <code>Control</code>, which calls a pure function
      in <code>crates/app/src/breaker.rs</code> and, on a rise, raises the admission flag in the
      same transaction, so the verdict and the flag cannot disagree.
    </p>
    <p>
      The steps are two. A window over a meter’s line, or priced over the dollar line, sets{' '}
      <code>read-only</code>. A window priced over four times the dollar line, $0.1389 and about a
      week of the ceiling, or still over a line after a trip, sets <code>off</code>, and the Worker
      then enables the pre-built WAF rule that blocks the API hostname, through the Rulesets API.
      The state only goes up. A person resets it, in Data Studio and the dashboard, once the cause
      is fixed. An automatic reset after quiet windows would let a loop start again every few hours.
    </p>

    <h2>The nuances of the tech</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The analytics lag, so the slot before.</strong> Cloudflare’s Workers page, read on{' '}
        {READ}, warns of a slight delay in aggregation and gives no number. The current slot would
        look empty, so the breaker reads the one that has ended. The price is time: a loop runs for
        up to 30 minutes before the first run that can see it. A zero for Worker or Durable Object
        requests is logged as empty, because the cron’s own run leaves at least one of each, so a
        zero there means late data, not a quiet account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two field names nobody at Cloudflare documents.</strong> The page for the periodic
        Durable Object dataset documents only <code>cpuTime</code> and points to introspection. The
        names for rows written and duration, <code>rowsWritten</code> and <code>duration</code>,
        come from a third-party dump of the schema. If they are wrong, Cloudflare answers with a
        validation error, and the two meters are marked unread and shown on the status page. The
        price is then a floor, and the other three meters still count.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One request per dataset.</strong> A GraphQL validation error fails the whole
        request. Asking for all five meters at once would let one wrong name hide the other four, so
        each dataset has its own request and its own five-second timeout.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A rule update wants every field.</strong> The update page, read on {READ}, says to
        include all the rule fields wanted in the new definition, so the breaker reads the rule
        first and sends its action, expression and description back with <code>enabled: true</code>.
        Whether <code>enabled</code> alone would do is not tested.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A zero ceiling goes straight to off.</strong> Any priced usage is past a line of
        zero, so the launch checklist’s forced trip, with <code>BREAKER_CEILING_USD</code> set to
        “0”, skips read-only and enables the real kill rule. Run it only when someone is ready to
        reset it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The whole account, every count billable.</strong> The ceiling belongs to the
        account, so the breaker prices the site’s lab Worker as well, and counts every unit as past
        the free allowance. It trips earlier than the invoice would, which is the safe side: a false
        trip is reset by hand, and a late one is not undone.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>
      Each run is 4 requests to Cloudflare, 384 a day, and one request to <code>Control</code> and
      about one row, which <code>docs/cost.md</code> counts in its idle month: 2,910 Worker requests
      with the wipes, 0.03% of those included. The API Worker’s wasm was 4,400,280 bytes on
      2026-11-06, against a budget of 5,317,776 (<code>docs/roadmap.md</code>, PR 17). The larger
      cost is the reach of a false trip. Read-only turns away new work at the door, and off blocks
      the API hostname, so the public pages stay but the signed-in screens go offline. There is no
      email yet, so the signals are the status page’s Breaker line and a morning issue.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the breaker cannot do is stop an alarm: neither the flag nor the rule reaches a loop
      inside an object, so the loop guard bounds each object to 100 re-arms a day. And it has met no
      real usage yet. The field names, the token’s permission names and the update body are listed
      in <code>docs/cost.md</code> as open until the first real run.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Read <a href="/status/">the status page’s</a> Breaker line, then the same state in{' '}
      <code>GET /v1/status</code>. To watch every path, run <code>scripts/smoke-breaker.sh</code>:
      it starts a fake of the two Cloudflare APIs and walks 114 checks through unconfigured, armed,
      read-only, off with the rule enabled, held while tripped, a rule enable that fails and is
      retried, an unread meter, a request that times out and a rejected token. Its last phase sets
      the ceiling to “0”. In the log, <code>breaker_read</code> lines give each window’s verdict and
      price, and <code>breaker_meter_unread</code> says which meter and why.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li>
        <em>Release It!</em>, 2nd edition (book, 2018): the circuit breaker and the shedding of
        load, as the bakery’s <code>docs/architecture.md</code> cites them. The book was not read on{' '}
        {READ}.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08031" rel="noopener">
          A paper on denial of wallet
        </a>
        , arXiv, 2021: the attack, or the bug, that forces financial exhaustion on a serverless
        account, as <code>docs/cost.md</code> defines it. The paper was not read on {READ}.
      </li>
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">
            {s.title}
          </a>
          , {s.by}, read on {READ}: {s.for}.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="hint">
      Back to <a href={howUrl('breaker')}>the breaker principle on How it’s built</a>, to the
      decision that chose it,{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0010')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0010
      </a>
      , or to{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        the lab
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
